  <section class="faith-intro">
    <div class="container container-narrow">
      <details class="faith-intro-disclosure" data-faith-intro>
        <summary class="faith-intro-summary">
          <span class="faith-intro-summary-label" data-faith-intro-label>Read introduction</span>
          <span class="faith-intro-summary-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </summary>
        <div class="faith-intro-body">
          <p class="faith-intro-prose hero-excerpt-dropcap">The 1928 Book of Common Prayer is the last revision of the American Prayer Book before the controversial 1979 edition that replaced it. It contains the full liturgical life of the Anglican Church: the Daily Office of Morning and Evening Prayer, the Holy Communion, the sacramental rites of Baptism, Confirmation, Matrimony, and the Visitation of the Sick, the Burial of the Dead, the Ordinal for making deacons, priests, and bishops, the Psalter in Coverdale’s translation arranged for the 30-day cycle, and the prayers and thanksgivings that mark the occasions of ordinary life. The daily office preserves the structure Cranmer laid down in 1549. This edition presents the original Elizabethan English alongside a modernized reading for those who prefer contemporary language, while preserving the cadence and theology of the text.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>
    
